WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:07.732
Spotify wants the average rating per genre across a hundred billion listens.

00:00:07.732 --> 00:00:13.464
On the row storage you just saw, that reads every byte of every row and takes hours.

00:00:13.464 --> 00:00:19.196
BigQuery answers it in seconds, and the whole trick is in this picture.

00:00:19.196 --> 00:00:24.928
First, the table is stored by column, so the query reads only the rating and genre columns and skips the other eighteen.

00:00:24.928 --> 00:00:30.660
Next, that scan splits across thousands of workers that exist only while the query runs.

00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:36.392
Finally, their partial answers bubble up to a root that combines them into the final number.

00:00:36.392 --> 00:00:42.124
Read only the columns you touch, on machines you rent by the second.

00:00:42.124 --> 00:00:45.979
Where did this come from.

00:00:45.979 --> 00:00:49.833
In two thousand five, a Google engineer named Andrey Gubarev needed answers from the web crawl.

00:00:49.833 --> 00:00:53.688
The queries ran for hours, eating into his foosball time.

00:00:53.688 --> 00:00:57.542
So he dug into why they were slow.

00:00:57.542 --> 00:01:01.397
That investigation became Dremel, and Dremel shipped to the world as BigQuery.

00:01:01.397 --> 00:01:06.772
The slow part was the layout.

00:01:06.772 --> 00:01:12.148
Each web page was one row, and most of that row was the raw page content, megabytes per record.

00:01:12.148 --> 00:01:17.523
Ask a simple question, like trends by date, and row storage still drags the whole page off disk just to read a few bytes of date.

00:01:17.523 --> 00:01:22.899
Across billions of pages, that is hours of wasted reading.

00:01:22.899 --> 00:01:26.816
Dremel flipped the layout.

00:01:26.816 --> 00:01:30.732
Instead of keeping each row together, keep each column together.

00:01:30.732 --> 00:01:34.648
All the dates in one file, all the bodies in another.

00:01:34.648 --> 00:01:38.565
Now a query by date opens only the date file and never touches the body.

00:01:38.565 --> 00:01:42.481
A column of similar values also compresses by another order of magnitude, because genres and ratings repeat.

00:01:42.481 --> 00:01:46.398
Hours became seconds.

00:01:46.398 --> 00:01:51.209
Put real numbers on it.

00:01:51.209 --> 00:01:56.020
The table is one terabyte, twenty columns, and the query needs just two, rating and genre.

00:01:56.020 --> 00:02:00.832
Row storage reads the whole terabyte in over ten minutes.

00:02:00.832 --> 00:02:05.643
BigQuery reads about fifty gigabytes, compressed and spread across a hundred workers, in fifteen seconds.

00:02:05.643 --> 00:02:10.454
Twenty times less data off disk, forty times faster.

00:02:10.454 --> 00:02:14.951
That same pattern, columns plus compression plus distribution, now powers Redshift, Snowflake, Parquet, and ClickHouse.

00:02:14.951 --> 00:02:19.448
The lesson is that BigQuery is the workload opposite of Postgres.

00:02:19.448 --> 00:02:23.945
Row storage wins when you grab one whole record.

00:02:23.945 --> 00:02:28.442
Column storage wins when you scan a few fields across billions of rows.

00:02:28.442 --> 00:02:32.939
Pick the layout that matches your queries.

00:02:32.939 --> 00:02:37.436
Next, when you need both at once, hybrid storage.
